proof:
Conventions. ℝ^k = Eucl k = EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin k) with inner product ⟨·,·⟩ and norm |·|; Lⁿ = volume on ℝⁿ; Hⁿ := μHE[n] on ℝ^P. e_i := EuclideanSpace.single i 1, D := fderiv ℝ, and JΦ(w) := √det(⟨DΦ(w)e_i, DΦ(w)e_j⟩)_{i,j<n} (Gram-determinant Jacobian; in Lean Real.sqrt (Matrix.of fun i j => inner ℝ (fderiv ℝ Φ w (EuclideanSpace.single i 1)) (fderiv ℝ Φ w (EuclideanSpace.single j 1))).det). S′ := B ∩ χ⁻¹(S). Fix K and c > 0 from the hypotheses.

This is steps 4–6 of the parent proof (slicing_im_creg_box_coarea), for a general dimension n and a general Borel weight ψ; the parent applies it with n := m+1 and ψ := ‖G·‖ₑ·g. The injectivity of χ on B, which the parent derives in its step 2, is a hypothesis here. Sibling lemma used: area_formula (Submission.lpf_raf_chart_change_of_variables). Mathlib results used: Lusin–Souslin MeasurableSet.image_of_continuousOn_injOn, ContinuousOn.measurable_piecewise and the change of variables formula MeasureTheory.lintegral_image_eq_lintegral_abs_det_fderiv_mul.

1. (Φ is injective and continuous on Ω.) Let w, w′ ∈ Ω with Φ(w) = Φ(w′). Then c|w − w′| ≤ |Φ(w) − Φ(w′)| = 0. If |w − w′| > 0, then c|w − w′| > 0 since c > 0, a contradiction. So |w − w′| ≤ 0 and w = w′. Φ is continuous on Ω because it is K-Lipschitz there (LipschitzOnWith.continuousOn).
2. (Φ(S) is Borel and Ω ∩ Φ⁻¹(Φ(S)) = S.) We have S ⊆ χ(B) ⊆ Ω. Φ is continuous and injective on the Borel set S ⊆ Ω (step 1) and ℝⁿ is Polish, so Φ(S) is Borel by Lusin–Souslin (MeasurableSet.image_of_continuousOn_injOn). For the set identity: if w ∈ S, then w ∈ Ω and Φ(w) ∈ Φ(S). Conversely, if w ∈ Ω and Φ(w) = Φ(w′) with w′ ∈ S, then w′ ∈ Ω, and injectivity on Ω gives w = w′ ∈ S.
3. (Area formula for Φ.) Apply area_formula with P, n, Φ, Ω, A := Φ(S) and weight ψ. Its hypotheses hold:
- Ω is open, and Φ is K-Lipschitz and c-antilipschitz on Ω;
- A ⊆ Φ(Ω), because S ⊆ Ω;
- A is Borel (step 2), hence Hⁿ-null-measurable;
- ψ is Borel, hence a.e.-measurable for Hⁿ⌞A.
Its third conclusion, rewritten with Ω ∩ Φ⁻¹(Φ(S)) = S from step 2, reads ∫_{Φ(S)} ψ dHⁿ = ∫_S ψ(Φ w)·ofReal(JΦ(w)) dLⁿ(w).
4. (S′ is Borel.) χ is C¹ on B, hence continuous on B (ContDiffOn.continuousOn). Let χ̃ := B.piecewise χ 0, which is χ on B and 0 off B. It is Borel measurable by ContinuousOn.measurable_piecewise, since χ is continuous on the measurable set B and the constant 0 is continuous on Bᶜ. As χ̃ = χ on B, S′ = B ∩ χ⁻¹(S) = B ∩ χ̃⁻¹(S), which is Borel because B is open.
5. (χ(S′) = S.) If y ∈ S′, then χ(y) ∈ S. Conversely, if w ∈ S ⊆ χ(B), then w = χ(y) for some y ∈ B, and this y lies in S′.
6. (Derivative of χ on S′.) Let y ∈ S′ ⊆ B. Since χ is C¹ on B, it is differentiable within B at y (ContDiffOn.differentiableOn with 1 ≠ 0). As B is open, B is a neighbourhood of y, so χ is differentiable at y and HasFDerivAt χ (Dχ(y)) y holds. In particular HasFDerivWithinAt χ (Dχ(y)) S′ y. Also, χ is injective on S′ because S′ ⊆ B.
7. (Change of variables along χ.) Apply lintegral_image_eq_lintegral_abs_det_fderiv_mul with:
- μ := volume on ℝⁿ, an additive Haar measure;
- the Borel set S′ (step 4);
- f := χ and f′ := Dχ (step 6), with χ injective on S′ (step 6);
- the function G₀(w) := ψ(Φ w)·ofReal(JΦ(w)).
It gives ∫_{χ(S′)} G₀ dLⁿ = ∫_{S′} ofReal|det Dχ(y)|·G₀(χ y) dLⁿ(y). By step 5 the left side is ∫_S G₀ dLⁿ.
8. (Conclusion.) Steps 3 and 7 together give ∫_{Φ(S)} ψ dHⁿ = ∫_{S′} ofReal|det Dχ(y)|·(ψ(Φ(χ y))·ofReal(JΦ(χ y))) dLⁿ(y). S′ is Borel (step 4), so lintegral_indicator turns this set integral into the integral over ℝⁿ of (B ∩ χ⁻¹(S)).indicator (y ↦ ofReal|det Dχ(y)|·(ψ(Φ(χ y))·ofReal(JΦ(χ y)))). This is the claimed identity. ∎
